{
  "name": "What Happens During a Senior Home Safety Assessment?",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Ever wonder what goes on during a senior home safety assessment? It's pretty straightforward, but super effective. It usually starts with a trained professional doing a complete room-by-room walk-through of your home. They're looking for all sorts of potential hazards — things like trip risks from loose rugs or cords, areas with poor lighting, and how safe your bathroom is. They’ll check if grab bars are needed or if existing ones are secure, and even look at things like water temperature settings. After the walk-through, they'll typically give you a written report. This report usually includes prioritized recommendations, pointing out the most important things to fix first, along with practical suggestions. Sometimes, this assessment happens before or after a fall, or even after a hospital discharge, which makes it even more critical. The goal is to catch those small, easily missed hazards — like unsecured grab bars or lack of non-skid surfaces — before they become a bigger problem. It's all about making your home a safer, more comfortable place to live as you age.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
